he collection is consistently >30s would it flip, but then context timeout cancels at 30s and returns error → stale (correctly, because the collection is timing out). So actually the 30s timeout means a collection can't \"legitimately\" exceed 30s; it would error out and be marked stale. So the boundary is consistent.\n\nWait, subtle: if the collection is cancelled at 30s, `runJSON` returns error, so recordRefresh sets Err, and classifyFreshness returns Stale (immediately, regardless of window). So a collection exceeding 30s → error → stale. So the \"legitimate\" gap is strictly ≤ interval + 30s. The window of interval + 31s always covers it with 1s slack. Good.\n\n**Edge case 2**: What about `LastSuccess` being updated only on success. If a worker is healthy but the collection occasionally fails (transient error), then Err is set → stale immediately. That's pre-existing behavior (Err → Stale). The change doesn't affect this.\n\n**Edge case 3**: The test `TestFreshnessCoversFleetAndIntervalChanges` checks fleet instance \"two\" is fresh at 35s and stale at 41s (fleetWindow). And selected instance \"one\" at 35s is stale (selected window 33s). Consistent.\n\n**Edge case 4**: `seedFreshnessSuccess` sets `state.Snapshot = &Snapshot{}` then re-assigns `state.Snapshot = &snapshot`. The first assignment is dead code (creates a pointer then overwrites). Minor, in test only. Not blocking.\n\nLet me check: \n```go\nstate.Snapshot = &Snapshot{}\nsnapshot := freshnessTestSnapshot(instance)\nstate.Snapshot = &snapshot\n```\nYes, `state.Snapshot = &Snapshot{}` is immediately overwritten. Dead code, cosmetic. Not blocking.\n\n**Edge case 5**: In `TestFreshnessCycleBudgetSelectedWindow`, the app is `NewApp(inventory, &testCollector{}, nil)` — the collector is `&testCollector{}` with no collect function, so if a refresh were triggered, Collect returns `Snapshot{Instance: instance}` with no Version/Config/Status. But this test never starts the app or triggers refresh; it only uses `seedFreshnessSuccess` and `pageView`. So fine. The `testCollector` is only used as a placeholder.\n\nActually wait — `pageView` with a nil templates? `app.pageView` doesn't use templates. `renderInstanceFreshness` loads templates separately. So fine.\n\n**Edge case 6**: `renderInstanceFreshness(t, app.pageView(...).Selected)` — `app.pageView` requires `now` non-zero (it uses `time.Now()` if zero, but here it passes base.Add(...)). Fine.\n\n**Edge case 7**: `formatTime(base)` produces \"2026-09-04 12:00:00Z\". `seedFreshnessSuccess` sets LastSuccess = base (time.Date 2026-09-04 12:00:00 UTC). view.LastObserved = formatTime(base) = \"2026-09-04 12:00:00Z\". Test asserts equality. Good.\n\n**Edge case 8**: Does `classifyFreshness` boundary behave as test expects? \n```go\nif maxAge > 0 && !now.Before(state.LastSuccess.Add(maxAge)) {\n    return Stale\n}\n```\nAt now = base + selectedWindow (exactly), `now.Before(LastSuccess.Add(maxAge))` = false, so returns Stale. At now = base + selectedWindow - 1ms, now.Before(...) = true, returns Fresh. Test checks `selectedWindow - time.Millisecond` fresh and `selectedWindow` stale. Correct.\n\n**Edge case 9**: `freshnessWindow` uses `refreshTimeout` (30s). But note `refreshTimeout` is also used as the collector context timeout. Is there any coupling issue if `refreshTimeout` were changed? Not in this diff.\n\nNow let me look at the templates to confirm `status-badge fresh` etc. exist, and the fleet.html `state-dot fresh`.","thinkingSignature":"[{\"type\":\"reasoning.text\",\"text\":\"`collector := NewCLICollector(refreshTimeout)` — so the collector's timeout is 30s, matching refreshTimeout. Good, consistent.\\n\\nSo the freshnessWindow of interval + 30s + 1s is consistent with the actual bound of a legitimate cycle.\\n\\nNow, let me think about edge cases in the change more deeply.\\n\\n**Edge case 1**: What if `refreshInterval` returns a very large value (e.g., configured 86400 seconds = 1 day)? Then freshnessWindow = 1 day + 30s + 1s. Previously 3 days. So the new threshold is actually SHORTER than the old 3x for intervals > ~30s. Wait: old = 3*interval. New = interval + 31s. For interval = 86400s, old = 259200s (3 days), new = 86431s (1 day). So the new window is much SHORTER for large intervals.\\n\\nIs that a problem? The comment in the code and commit say \\\"derive freshness window from collection cycle budget\\\". The semantic is: a silent worker is stale after one legitimate cycle. For a fleet instance with 1-day interval, one legitimate cycle is 1 day + 30s collection + slack. So stale after ~1 day. Previously it would be stale after 3 days. This is more aggressive for large intervals but arguably more correct (one missed cycle = stale, not three).\\n\\nIs there a behavioral concern? For very short intervals (selected 2s), the new window (33s) is much longer than old (6s). So it's more lenient for short intervals, more strict for long intervals. This is a deliberate design choice documented in the commit message: \\\"derive freshness window from collection cycle budget.\\\"\\n\\nIs there any scenario where the new window is too aggressive for long intervals? Consider a fleet instance with interval 300s (5 min). Old threshold = 900s (15 min). New = 331s (~5.5 min). A collection that takes up to 30s means legitimate gap = 330s, and window = 331s. So if the collector takes the full 30s, the gap between successes is 330s, and window is 331s — 1s slack. So a healthy instance where each collection takes 30s (max) would be right at the boundary. But that's a pathological case (every collection at max 30s).\\n\\nActually wait, more carefully: for interval=300s, the loop: success at t=0. timer reset 300s. fires at t=300. collection takes 30s. success at t=330. Next success gap = 330s. Window = 331s. So the instance stays fresh (330 < 331). But if there's any timer drift >1s, it would flip to stale right before the next success. Hmm. But the slack is 1s. Is 1s enough?\\n\\nActually, let me reconsider. The comment says \\\"freshnessSchedulingSlack is the small scheduling allowance included in the missed-refresh budget for timer and render drift.\\\" The render drift is the time between pageView's `now` and actual. But this is a soft operator indicator, so minor flakiness between fresh/stale at the boundary is not a correctness issue. The test pins the boundary at exactly the window value (stale at ==window). This is deterministic in tests since they use synthetic times.\\n\\nBut for a real deployment, is there a risk that the freshness indicator oscillates? If an instance is healthy and collections take, say, 5s each with 2s interval: gap = 2s + 5s = 7s. Window = 33s. So it stays fresh comfortably. If collections take near 30s every time, the gap ≈ 32s, window 33s, so it stays fresh with 1s margin. Only if the collection is consistently >30s would it flip, but then context timeout cancels at 30s and returns error → stale (correctly, because the collection is timing out). So actually the 30s timeout means a collection can't \\\"legitimately\\\" exceed 30s; it would error out and be marked stale. So the boundary is consistent.\\n\\nWait, subtle: if the collection is cancelled at 30s, `runJSON` returns error, so recordRefresh sets Err, and classifyFreshness returns Stale (immediately, regardless of window). So a collection exceeding 30s → error → stale. So the \\\"legitimate\\\" gap is strictly ≤ interval + 30s. The window of interval + 31s always covers it with 1s slack. Good.\\n\\n**Edge case 2**: What about `LastSuccess` being updated only on success. If a worker is healthy but the collection occasionally fails (transient error), then Err is set → stale immediately. That's pre-existing behavior (Err → Stale). The change doesn't affect this.\\n\\n**Edge case 3**: The test `TestFreshnessCoversFleetAndIntervalChanges` checks fleet instance \\\"two\\\" is fresh at 35s and stale at 41s (fleetWindow). And selected instance \\\"one\\\" at 35s is stale (selected window 33s). Consistent.\\n\\n**Edge case 4**: `seedFreshnessSuccess` sets `state.Snapshot = &Snapshot{}` then re-assigns `state.Snapshot = &snapshot`. The first assignment is dead code (creates a pointer then overwrites). Minor, in test only. Not blocking.\\n\\nLet me check: \\n```go\\nstate.Snapshot = &Snapshot{}\\nsnapshot := freshnessTestSnapshot(instance)\\nstate.Snapshot = &snapshot\\n```\\nYes, `state.Snapshot = &Snapshot{}` is immediately overwritten. Dead code, cosmetic. Not blocking.\\n\\n**Edge case 5**: In `TestFreshnessCycleBudgetSelectedWindow`, the app is `NewApp(inventory, &testCollector{}, nil)` — the collector is `&testCollector{}` with no collect function, so if a refresh were triggered, Collect returns `Snapshot{Instance: instance}` with no Version/Config/Status. But this test never starts the app or triggers refresh; it only uses `seedFreshnessSuccess` and `pageView`. So fine. The `testCollector` is only used as a placeholder.\\n\\nActually wait — `pageView` with a nil templates? `app.pageView` doesn't use templates. `renderInstanceFreshness` loads templates separately. So fine.\\n\\n**Edge case 6**: `renderInstanceFreshness(t, app.pageView(...).Selected)` — `app.pageView` requires `now` non-zero (it uses `time.Now()` if zero, but here it passes base.Add(...)). Fine.\\n\\n**Edge case 7**: `formatTime(base)` produces \\\"2026-09-04 12:00:00Z\\\". `seedFreshnessSuccess` sets LastSuccess = base (time.Date 2026-09-04 12:00:00 UTC). view.LastObserved = formatTime(base) = \\\"2026-09-04 12:00:00Z\\\". Test asserts equality. Good.\\n\\n**Edge case 8**: Does `classifyFreshness` boundary behave as test expects? \\n```go\\nif maxAge > 0 && !now.Before(state.LastSuccess.Add(maxAge)) {\\n    return Stale\\n}\\n```\\nAt now = base + selectedWindow (exactly), `now.Before(LastSuccess.Add(maxAge))` = false, so returns Stale. At now = base + selectedWindow - 1ms, now.Before(...) = true, returns Fresh. Test checks `selectedWindow - time.Millisecond` fresh and `selectedWindow` stale. Correct.\\n\\n**Edge case 9**: `freshnessWindow` uses `refreshTimeout` (30s). But note `refreshTimeout` is also used as the collector context timeout. Is there any coupling issue if `refreshTimeout` were changed? Not in this diff.\\n\\nNow let me look at the templates to confirm `status-badge fresh` etc. exist, and the fleet.html `state-dot fresh`.\",\"format\":\"unknown\",\"index\":0}]"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_wni6qz57c24nufd8yx7177n6","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/phaedrus/development/misty-step/canopy/.forest/worktrees/1788551384472524279-verifier && ls -la templates static && echo \"=== instance.html ===\" && cat templates/instance.html && echo \"=== fleet.html ===\" && cat templates/fleet.html"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"openrouter","model":"deepseek/deepseek-v4-pro-0813","usage":{"input":1756,"output":1832,"cacheRead":47360,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":1725,"totalTokens":50948,"cost":{"input":0.0011589600000000001,"output":0.00362736,"cacheRead":0.00104192,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.00582824}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1788551637601,"responseId":"gen-1788551637-rW5DKo9i9A1lGr89wL0X","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_wni6qz57c24nufd8yx7177n6","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"static:\ntotal 64\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 phaedrus phaedrus    42 Sep  4 14:49 .\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 phaedrus phaedrus   566 Sep  4 14:52 ..\n-rw-r--r-- 1 phaedrus phaedrus  8991 Sep  4 14:49 canopy.css\n-rw-r--r-- 1 phaedrus phaedrus 51332 Sep  4 14:49 htmx.min.js\n\ntemplates:\ntotal 24\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 phaedrus phaedrus   80 Sep  4 14:49 .\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 phaedrus phaedrus  566 Sep  4 14:52 ..\n-rw-r--r-- 1 phaedrus phaedrus 1808 Sep  4 14:49 fleet.html\n-rw-r--r-- 1 phaedrus phaedrus 9407 Sep  4 14:49 instance.html\n-rw-r--r-- 1 phaedrus phaedrus 1054 Sep  4 14:49 log.html\n-rw-r--r-- 1 phaedrus phaedrus  844 Sep  4 14:49 page.html\n=== instance.html ===\n<section class=\"instance-panel\" id=\"instance-panel\"\n  {{if .ID}}hx-get=\"/fragments/instance?instance={{.ID}}\" hx-trigger=\"every 2s [document.hidden === false]\" hx-target=\"#instance-panel\" hx-swap=\"outerHTML\"{{end}}>\n  {{if .ID}}\n    <header class=\"identity\">\n      <div>\n        <p class=\"eyebrow\">Selected instance</p>\n        <h2>{{if .Label}}{{.Label}}{{else}}{{.ID}}{{end}}</h2>\n        <p class=\"identity-meta\"><span class=\"mono\">{{.ID}}</span>{{if .Host}} · {{.Host}}{{end}}{{if .Repo}} · {{.Repo}}{{end}}</p>\n      </div>\n      <span class=\"status-badge {{.Freshness}}\">{{.Freshness}}</span>\n      {{if .LastObserved}}<span class=\"identity-meta\">last observed {{.LastObserved}}</span>{{end}}\n    </header>\n\n    {{if .Errors}}\n      <section class=\"card card-wide\" aria-labelledby=\"instance-errors\">\n        <div class=\"card-header\"><h2 id=\"instance-errors\">Collection warnings</h2><span class=\"signal error\">attention</span></div>\n        <ul class=\"error-list\">\n          {{range .Errors}}<li>{{.}}</li>{{end}}\n        </ul>\n      </section>\n    {{end}}\n\n    {{if eq .Freshness \"unknown\"}}\n      <section class=\"card card-wide\" aria-labelledby=\"waiting-title\">\n        <div class=\"card-header\"><h2 id=\"waiting-title\">Waiting for first observation</h2><span class=\"signal unknown\">unknown</span></div>\n        <p class=\"muted\">No successful snapshot is available yet. Canopy will keep trying without treating this instance as healthy.</p>\n      </section>\n    {{else}}\n      <div class=\"card-grid\">\n        <section class=\"card card-third\" aria-labelledby=\"overview-title\">\n          <div class=\"card-header\"><h2 id=\"overview-title\">Overview</h2><span class=\"signal {{.Freshness}}\">{{.Freshness}}</span></div>\n          <dl class=\"metric-row\">\n            <div class=\"metric\"><dt>Kernel</dt><dd>{{if .KernelKnown}}{{if .KernelRunning}}running{{else}}stopped{{end}}{{else}}unknown{{end}}</dd></div>\n            <div class=\"metric\"><dt>Live runs</dt><dd>{{.ActiveRuns}}</dd></div>\n            <div class=\"metric\"><dt>Build</dt><dd class=\"mono\">{{.Version}}</dd></div>\n          </dl>\n        </section>\n        <section class=\"card card-third\" aria-labelledby=\"audit-title\">\n          <div class=\"card-header\"><h2 id=\"audit-title\">Audit trust</h2><span class=\"signal {{.Audit.TrustClass}}\">{{.Audit.Trust}}</span></div>\n          <dl class=\"metric-row\">\n            <div class=\"metric\"><dt>Result</dt><dd>{{if .Audit.LastResult}}{{.Audit.LastResult}}{{else}}unknown{{end}}</dd></div>\n            <div class=\"metric\"><dt>Audited</dt><dd class=\"mono\">{{if .Audit.AuditedMaster}}{{.Audit.AuditedMaster}}{{else}}unknown{{end}}</dd></div>\n          </dl>\n          {{if .Audit.Violations}}<ul class=\"error-list\">{{range .Audit.Violations}}<li>{{.}}</li>{{end}}</ul>{{end}}\n        </section>\n        <section class=\"card card-third\" aria-labelledby=\"config-summary-title\">\n          <div class=\"card-header\"><h2 id=\"config-summary-title\">Configuration</h2><span class=\"muted mono\">{{.Config.Primary}}</span></div>\n          <p class=\"muted\">{{if .Config.PrimarySource}}primary from {{.Config.PrimarySource}}{{else}}primary declaration{{end}}</p>\n          {{if .Config.Scope.Label}}<p><strong>Scope:</strong> {{.Config.Scope.Label}}</p>{{end}}\n          {{if .Config.Scope.BranchPrefix}}<p><strong>Branch:</strong> <span class=\"mono\">{{.Config.Scope.BranchPrefix}}</span></p>{{end}}\n          {{if .Config.Scope.Subjects}}<p><strong>Subjects:</strong> {{len .Config.Scope.Subjects}}</p>{{end}}\n        </section>\n\n        <section class=\"card card-wide\" aria-labelledby=\"live-title\">\n          <div class=\"card-header\"><h2 id=\"live-title\">Live work</h2><span class=\"muted\">{{.ActiveRuns}} active</span></div>\n          {{if .LiveRuns}}\n            <div class=\"data-table-wrap\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Run</th><th>Agent</th><th>Started</th><th>Elapsed</th><th>Logs</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n              {{range .LiveRuns}}\n                <tr><td class=\"mono\">{{.RunID}}</td><td>{{if .Agent}}{{.Agent}}{{else}}legacy / unnamed{{end}}</td><td class=\"mono\">{{.StartedAt}}</td><td>{{.Elapsed}}</td><td><a class=\"log-link\" href=\"/logs?instance={{$.ID}}&amp;run={{.RunID}}\" hx-get=\"/logs?instance={{$.ID}}&amp;run={{.RunID}}\" hx-target=\"next .log-output\" hx-swap=\"outerHTML\">open log</a><div id=\"log-{{.RunID}}\" class=\"log-output muted\" hx-preserve=\"true\">on demand</div></td></tr>\n              {{end}}\n            </tbody></table></div>\n          {{else}}<p class=\"empty\">No live runs reported.</p>{{end}}\n        </section>\n\n        <section class=\"card card-half\" aria-labelledby=\"trigger-title\">\n          <div class=\"card-header\"><h2 id=\"trigger-title\">Trigger matrix</h2><span class=\"muted\">poll vs run</span></div>\n          {{if .Triggers}}\n            <div class=\"data-table-wrap\">\n            <table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Agent</th><th>Poll</th><th>Run</th><th>Errors</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n              {{range .Triggers}}\n                <tr><td>{{if .Name}}{{.Name}}{{else}}legacy / unnamed{{end}}</td><td><span class=\"signal {{.PollStatus}}\">{{.PollStatus}}</span>{{if .PollError}}<br><span class=\"muted\">{{.PollError}}</span>{{end}}</td><td><span class=\"signal {{.RunStatus}}\">{{.RunStatus}}</span>{{if .RunError}}<br><span class=\"muted\">{{.RunError}}</span>{{end}}</td><td>{{.ConsecutiveErrors}}</td></tr>\n              {{end}}\n            </tbody></table>\n            </div>\n          {{else}}<p class=\"empty\">No trigger state reported.</p>{{end}}\n        </section>\n\n        <section class=\"card card-half\" aria-labelledby=\"recent-title\">\n          <div class=\"card-header\"><h2 id=\"recent-title\">Recent runs</h2><span class=\"muted\">newest first</span></div>\n          {{if .RecentRuns}}\n            <div class=\"data-table-wrap\">\n            <table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Run</th><th>Agent</th><th>Outcome</th><th>Duration</th><th>Logs</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n              {{range .RecentRuns}}\n                <tr><td class=\"mono\">{{.RunID}}</td><td>{{if .Agent}}{{.Agent}}{{else}}legacy / unnamed{{end}}</td><td><span class=\"signal {{if eq .Status \"passed\"}}ok{{else}}error{{end}}\">{{.ExitLabel}}</span>{{if .Error}}<br><span class=\"muted\">{{.Error}}</span>{{end}}</td><td>{{.Duration}}</td><td><a class=\"log-link\" href=\"/logs?instance={{$.ID}}&amp;run={{.RunID}}\" hx-get=\"/logs?instance={{$.ID}}&amp;run={{.RunID}}\" hx-target=\"next .log-output\" hx-swap=\"outerHTML\">open</a><div id=\"log-{{.RunID}}\" class=\"log-output muted\" hx-preserve=\"true\">on demand</div></td></tr>\n              {{end}}\n            </tbody></table>\n            </div>\n          {{else}}<p class=\"empty\">No recent runs reported.</p>{{end}}\n        </section>\n\n        <section class=\"card card-wide\" aria-labelledby=\"ledger-title\">\n          <div class=\"card-header\">\n            <h2 id=\"ledger-title\">Ledger agents</h2>\n            {{if .HasLedger}}\n              <span class=\"muted\">{{.LedgerRuns}} runs · {{.LedgerPassRate}} pass rate</span>\n            {{else}}\n              <span class=\"signal unknown\">unsupported</span>\n            {{end}}\n          </div>\n          {{if .HasLedger}}\n            {{if .Agents}}\n              <div class=\"data-table-wrap\">\n              <table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Agent</th><th class=\"number\">Runs</th><th class=\"number\">Pass rate</th><th class=\"number\">P50</th><th class=\"number\">P95</th><th class=\"number\">Tokens in</th><th class=\"number\">Tokens out</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n                {{range .Agents}}\n                  <tr><td>{{if .Agent}}{{.Agent}}{{else}}legacy / unnamed{{end}}</td><td class=\"number\">{{.Runs}}</td><td class=\"number\">{{.PassRateLabel}}</td><td class=\"number\">{{.DurationP50Label}}</td><td class=\"number\">{{.DurationP95Label}}</td><td class=\"number\">{{.TokensIn}}</td><td class=\"number\">{{.TokensOut}}</td></tr>\n                {{end}}\n              </tbody></table>\n              </div>\n            {{else}}<p class=\"empty\">No Ledger agents recorded.</p>{{end}}\n          {{else}}\n            <p class=\"empty\">This Iron Forest build does not export status.ledger aggregates.</p>\n          {{end}}\n        </section>\n        <section class=\"card card-wide\" aria-labelledby=\"declarations-title\">\n          <div class=\"card-header\"><h2 id=\"declarations-title\">Declarations</h2><span class=\"muted\">configuration snapshot</span></div>\n          {{if .Config.Declarations}}\n            <div class=\"data-table-wrap\">\n            <table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Model</th><th>Model source</th><th>Tools</th><th>Watchdog</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n              {{range .Config.Declarations}}\n                <tr><td>{{if .Name}}{{.Name}}{{else}}legacy / unnamed{{end}}</td><td class=\"mono\">{{.Model}}</td><td>{{.ModelSource}}</td><td>{{range $index, $tool := .Tools}}{{if $index}}, {{end}}{{$tool}}{{end}}</td><td>{{if .MaxDuration}}{{.MaxDuration}}s{{else}}unbounded{{end}}</td></tr>\n              {{end}}\n            </tbody></table>\n            </div>\n          {{else}}<p class=\"empty\">No declarations reported.</p>{{end}}\n        </section>\n      </div>\n    {{end}}\n  {{else}}\n    <section class=\"card card-wide\" aria-labelledby=\"empty-title\"><div class=\"card-header\"><h2 id=\"empty-title\">No instance selected</h2><span class=\"signal unknown\">unknown</span></div><p class=\"empty\">Add an instance to the inventory to begin observing it.</p></section>\n  {{end}}\n</section>\n=== fleet.html ===\n<aside class=\"fleet-rail\" id=\"fleet\"\n  hx-get=\"/fragments/fleet\"\n  hx-trigger=\"every 10s [document.hidden === false], canopy-selection from:body\"\n  hx-target=\"#fleet\"\n  hx-swap=\"outerHTML\">\n  <a class=\"brand\" href=\"/\" aria-label=\"Canopy home\">\n    <span class=\"brand-mark\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⌁</span>\n    <span>\n      <span class=\"brand-name\">Canopy</span>\n      <span class=\"brand-caption\">Iron Forest view</span>\n    </span>\n  </a>\n  <p class=\"rail-heading\">Fleet</p>\n  {{if .Instances}}\n    <nav aria-label=\"Forest instances\">\n      <ul class=\"instance-list\">\n        {{range .Instances}}\n          <li>\n            <a class=\"instance-link\" href=\"/?instance={{.ID}}\"\n              hx-get=\"/fragments/instance?instance={{.ID}}\" hx-target=\"#instance-panel\" hx-push-url=\"/?instance={{.ID}}\"\n              aria-current=\"{{if .IsSelected}}true{{else}}false{{end}}\">\n              <span class=\"state-dot {{.Freshness}}\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span>\n              <span class=\"instance-name\">\n                <span class=\"instance-title-row\">\n                  <span class=\"instance-label\">{{if .Label}}{{.Label}}{{else}}{{.ID}}{{end}}</span>\n                  {{if .KernelKnown}}\n                    <span class=\"pill-badge {{if .KernelRunning}}running{{else}}stopped{{end}}\">{{if .KernelRunning}}live{{else}}stopped{{end}}</span>\n                  {{end}}\n                </span>\n                <span class=\"instance-meta\">\n                  {{if .Repo}}{{.Repo}}{{else}}{{.Freshness}}{{end}}\n                  {{if gt .ActiveRuns 0}} · <strong class=\"accent-text\">{{.ActiveRuns}} running</strong>{{end}}\n                </span>\n              </span>\n            </a>\n          </li>\n        {{end}}\n      </ul>\n    </nav>\n  {{else}}\n    <p class=\"rail-empty\">No instances configured.</p>\n  {{end}}\n</aside>\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1788551663871},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Templates use `status-badge {{.Freshness}}` and `state-dot {{.Freshness}}`. So the freshness value \"fresh\"/\"stale\"/\"unknown\" is used as class. Good.\n\nNow, let me think more deeply about potential correctness concerns in the change, focusing on the actual production behavior change.\n\nThe key question: is the new freshness window formula correct and safe? Let me think about the \"silent worker\" semantics.\n\nThe commit message: \"fix(canopy): derive freshness window from collection cycle budget\". The critic branch name is \"canopy-critic-stale-selected-window/freshness-cycle-budget\". So this is a fix in response to a critic finding about \"stale selected window\".\n\nLet me examine the previous behavior and whether there might be a remaining issue.\n\nOld: `3*a.refreshInterval(instance.ID)`.\n- Selected interval default 2s → stale after 6s.\n- Fleet interval default 10s → stale after 30s.\n\nThe problem with the old behavior: the refresh loop waits `interval` AFTER each collection completes. So the minimum time between two collection STARTS is interval + (collection duration). For a selected instance with a slow collection (say 5s), the time between starts is 2+5 = 7s. So a healthy selected instance would only update LastSuccess every ~7s, but the old threshold was 6s → it would be marked stale between updates (flapping fresh/stale). Even worse with collection 30s (2+30=32s gap), the old 6s threshold would mark it stale for most of the cycle.\n\nSo the fix makes the window = interval + collection bound + slack = one full legitimate cycle. This is correct.\n\nNow, is there any NEW bug introduced?\n\nLet me think about the `freshnessWindow` being applied to BOTH selected and fleet instances via `pageView`. Previously both used `3*refreshInterval`. Now both use the new formula. The fleet window changes from 30s to 41s (for default 10s). Selected changes from 6s to 33s. This is consistent.\n\nOne thing: The `refreshInterval(id)` function has a subtle behavior: it returns the selected interval for the selected instance and fleet interval otherwise. But `freshnessWindow` is called for each instance. For the selected instance, refreshInterval returns selected interval; for others, fleet interval. Correct.\n\nBut wait — there's a subtle issue. `refreshInterval(id)` reads `a.selected` under RLock. In `pageView`, we call `a.freshnessWindow(instance.ID)` in a loop. But `a.selected` could change between calls (concurrently). That's fine; it's just a snapshot of selection at render time.\n\nNow, let me consider: is `freshnessWindow` a method that acquires the lock once per instance? It calls `refreshInterval` which acquires RLock. There's no deadlock because `pageView` doesn't hold the lock while calling.\n\nLet me also double-check: is there any place else that computes freshness with the old `3*` formula that should also be updated? Let me grep.","thinkingSignature":"[{\"type\":\"reasoning.text\",\"text\":\"Templates use `status-badge {{.Freshness}}` and `state-dot {{.Freshness}}`. So the freshness value \\\"fresh\\\"/\\\"stale\\\"/\\\"unknown\\\" is used as class. Good.\\n\\nNow, let me think more deeply about potential correctness concerns in the change, focusing on the actual production behavior change.\\n\\nThe key question: is the new freshness window formula correct and safe? Let me think about the \\\"silent worker\\\" semantics.\\n\\nThe commit message: \\\"fix(canopy): derive freshness window from collection cycle budget\\\". The critic branch name is \\\"canopy-critic-stale-selected-window/freshness-cycle-budget\\\". So this is a fix in response to a critic finding about \\\"stale selected window\\\".\\n\\nLet me examine the previous behavior and whether there might be a remaining issue.\\n\\nOld: `3*a.refreshInterval(instance.ID)`.\\n- Selected interval default 2s → stale after 6s.\\n- Fleet interval default 10s → stale after 30s.\\n\\nThe problem with the old behavior: the refresh loop waits `interval` AFTER each collection completes. So the minimum time between two collection STARTS is interval + (collection duration). For a selected instance with a slow collection (say 5s), the time between starts is 2+5 = 7s. So a healthy selected instance would only update LastSuccess every ~7s, but the old threshold was 6s → it would be marked stale between updates (flapping fresh/stale). Even worse with collection 30s (2+30=32s gap), the old 6s threshold would mark it stale for most of the cycle.\\n\\nSo the fix makes the window = interval + collection bound + slack = one full legitimate cycle. This is correct.\\n\\nNow, is there any NEW bug introduced?\\n\\nLet me think about the `freshnessWindow` being applied to BOTH selected and fleet instances via `pageView`. Previously both used `3*refreshInterval`. Now both use the new formula. The fleet window changes from 30s to 41s (for default 10s). Selected changes from 6s to 33s. This is consistent.\\n\\nOne thing: The `refreshInterval(id)` function has a subtle behavior: it returns the selected interval for the selected instance and fleet interval otherwise. But `freshnessWindow` is called for each instance. For the selected instance, refreshInterval returns selected interval; for others, fleet interval. Correct.\\n\\nBut wait — there's a subtle issue. `refreshInterval(id)` reads `a.selected` under RLock. In `pageView`, we call `a.freshnessWindow(instance.ID)` in a loop. But `a.selected` could change between calls (concurrently). That's fine; it's just a snapshot of selection at render time.\\n\\nNow, let me consider: is `freshnessWindow` a method that acquires the lock once per instance? It calls `refreshInterval` which acquires RLock. There's no deadlock because `pageView` doesn't hold the lock while calling.\\n\\nLet me also double-check: is there any place else that computes freshness with the old `3*` formula that should also be updated? Let me grep.\",\"format\":\"unknown\",\"index\":0}]"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_pc3hh6dbecxe89jafbj5rc5h","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/phaedrus/development/misty-step/canopy/.forest/worktrees/1788551384472524279-verifier && grep -rn \"refreshInterval\\|c