rror":false,"timestamp":1788551683562},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The HEAD commit's parent is exactly master (674894f). So it can fast-forward master. Good.\n\nNow let me do a deeper verification of claims. The main claim is: \"derive freshness window from collection cycle budget\". Let me verify the formula is consistent with the actual refresh loop by re-reading refreshLoop carefully.\n\n```go\nfunc (a *App) refreshLoop(ctx context.Context, instance Instance, worker *instanceWorker) {\n\ttimer := time.NewTimer(0)\n\tdefer timer.Stop()\n\tfor {\n\t\tselect {\n\t\tcase <-ctx.Done():\n\t\t\treturn\n\t\tcase <-worker.wake:\n\t\t\ta.refreshOnce(ctx, instance)\n\t\tcase <-timer.C:\n\t\t\ta.refreshOnce(ctx, instance)\n\t\t}\n\t\tif !timer.Stop() {\n\t\t\tselect {\n\t\t\tcase <-timer.C:\n\t\t\tdefault:\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t\ttimer.Reset(a.refreshInterval(instance.ID))\n\t}\n}\n```\n\nNote: The timer is reset AFTER refreshOnce completes. So the time between two timer-driven refreshOnce starts = refreshOnce duration + interval. Since refreshOnce duration ≤ refreshTimeout (30s, via context timeout, assuming collector respects ctx), the gap ≤ 30s + interval.\n\nBut wait, there's a subtlety with the context timeout. In refreshOnce:\n```go\nctx, cancel = context.WithTimeout(ctx, refreshTimeout)\nsnapshot, err := a.collector.Collect(ctx, instance)\ncancel()\n```\n\nSo the collector is given a ctx with 30s timeout. The CLI collector (`cliCollector`) respects ctx via `exec.CommandContext(ctx, ...)` and its own timeout. So collection is bounded by 30s. Good.\n\nBut what about a custom collector that ignores ctx? The comment in app.go line 14-16 says: \"A collector should never hold a worker forever. The CLI collector also applies its own command timeout; this bound protects custom collectors.\" Hmm, actually the comment says the `refreshTimeout` bound \"protects custom collectors\" — but if a custom collector ignores ctx, the context.WithTimeout doesn't actually protect anything; the Collect call would block forever. The comment is a bit misleading, but that's pre-existing and not part of this diff.\n\nActually wait, the comment is:\n```go\n\t// A collector should never hold a worker forever. The CLI collector also\n\t// applies its own command timeout; this bound protects custom collectors.\n\trefreshTimeout = 30 * time.Second\n```\nThis is pre-existing. The new change adds the freshnessSchedulingSlack comment below. Not part of the review's introduced issues.\n\nSo the formula is consistent.\n\nNow, one more consideration: the freshnessWindow uses `refreshTimeout` = 30s. But is there a scenario where a legitimate collection could take longer than 30s WITHOUT returning an error? E.g., the CLI collector: if a single subcommand times out at 30s, `runJSON` returns an error → Collect returns error → Err set → stale. So no legitimate success beyond 30s. Good.\n\nBut wait — actually the `cliCollector.Collect` runs MULTIPLE subcommands sequentially (version, config show, declaration list, per-declaration show, status). Each subcommand is bounded by c.timeout (30s) AND the parent ctx (30s total). Since the parent ctx from refreshOnce has 30s deadline, the total Collect is bounded by 30s. So no success beyond 30s. Good.\n\nActually, let me double-check: `context.WithTimeout(ctx, refreshTimeout)` — ctx here is the parent passed from refreshLoop, which is the app's root ctx (no deadline). So WithTimeout creates a 30s deadline. Then inside Collect, `runJSON` does `context.WithTimeout(ctx, c.timeout)` where ctx is the 30s-deadline ctx. So child deadline = min(30s, now+30s) = 30s. So each subcommand is bounded by the remaining time until 30s. So total Collect ≤ 30s. \n\nSo the freshnessWindow = interval + 30s + 1s is exactly right for the production collector.\n\nNow let me verify the claim in the commit message more concretely, maybe by checking the git log of the branch or the critic's note. But it's fine.\n\nLet me consider whether there are any issues with the test file that could cause flaky CI. The `TestSlowSuccessRefreshRecoversWithoutOverlap` test:\n\n- Uses `time.After(5*time.Second)` for several waits, and a busy-spin. Could this be flaky under heavy load? The busy spin loop has a 5s deadline. After release is closed, the second Collect returns immediately, so state should update quickly. Should be fine.\n\nBut there's a subtle potential race in the test: After `close(collector.release)`, the first Collect (which was blocked on release) returns. Meanwhile, the test does:\n```go\nselect {\ncase <-collector.entered:\ncase <-time.After(5 * time.Second):\n    t.Fatal(\"follow-up collection did not start after release\")\n}\n```\nThis waits for the SECOND entry into Collect. But note: `entered` is a buffered channel of size 8. The first Collect sent one entry (consumed by the earlier `<-collector.entered`). The second Collect will send another entry. So this select waits for the second. Fine.\n\nBut wait — could the first Collect still be running when we `close(collector.release)`? Yes, it's blocked on `<-c.release`. Closing release unblocks it. It then returns `c.snapshot, nil`. Then defer decrements active. Then refreshOnce calls recordRefresh, then returns. Then refreshLoop resets timer and loops. The wake channel has 1 pending. Select picks wake → refreshOnce → second Collect → sends entered (second entry). So the test's second select receives the second entry. Good.\n\nActually, there's a subtle question: after the first Collect returns, does the refreshLoop process the wake before the timer? The timer was reset to 300s (won't fire). The wake channel has 1 buffered item. So select will pick wake. Good.\n\nSo the test is deterministic enough.\n\nNow, is there a concern that the test's busy-wait loop could consume a lot of CPU for up to 5s in a failure scenario? In a failure, the test would spin for 5s then fail. That's acceptable for a test. Not a production issue.\n\nNow let me consider code quality of the new test file. The `seedFreshnessSuccess` helper:\n```go\nfunc seedFreshnessSuccess(app *App, id string, at time.Time) {\n\tapp.mu.Lock()\n\tdefer app.mu.Unlock()\n\tstate := app.states[id]\n\tif state == nil {\n\t\tstate = &InstanceState{}\n\t\tapp.states[id] = state\n\t}\n\tvar instance Instance\n\tfor _, candidate := range app.inventory.Instances {\n\t\tif candidate.ID == id {\n\t\t\tinstance = candidate\n\t\t\tbreak\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tstate.Snapshot = &Snapshot{}\n\tsnapshot := freshnessTestSnapshot(instance)\n\tstate.Snapshot = &snapshot\n\tstate.LastSuccess = at\n\tstate.LastAttempt = at\n\tstate.Err = nil\n}\n```\n\nMinor: `state.Snapshot = &Snapshot{}` is dead assignment. Cosmetic. Also, if the instance ID isn't found, `instance` is zero-value Instance, so the snapshot's Instance field is empty. But all test IDs (\"one\", \"two\") exist. Fine.\n\nThe helper locks `app.mu` and iterates `app.inventory.Instances` — no lock conflict. Fine.\n\nNow, is there a concern that `seedFreshnessSuccess` sets `state.Snapshot` with a pointer to a local `snapshot`? That's fine; Go heap-allocates.\n\nNow let me think about whether the change introduces any issue with the `Reachable` field. `Reachable` = `state.Snapshot != nil && state.Err == nil`. It does not depend on freshness. So a stale instance with a snapshot and no error is still \"Reachable=true\" while \"Freshness=stale\". That's pre-existing semantics (stale ≠ unreachable). The test doesn't check Reachable. Fine.\n\nLet me also verify the whole test suite runs with `-race` to catch any data race in the new tests or production code. The forest.yaml checks don't include race, but as a reviewer I can run it to be thorough. Let me run `go test -race ./...`.","thinkingSignature":"[{\"type\":\"reasoning.text\",\"text\":\"The HEAD commit's parent is exactly master (674894f). So it can fast-forward master. Good.\\n\\nNow let me do a deeper verification of claims. The main claim is: \\\"derive freshness window from collection cycle budget\\\". Let me verify the formula is consistent with the actual refresh loop by re-reading refreshLoop carefully.\\n\\n```go\\nfunc (a *App) refreshLoop(ctx context.Context, instance Instance, worker *instanceWorker) {\\n\\ttimer := time.NewTimer(0)\\n\\tdefer timer.Stop()\\n\\tfor {\\n\\t\\tselect {\\n\\t\\tcase <-ctx.Done():\\n\\t\\t\\treturn\\n\\t\\tcase <-worker.wake:\\n\\t\\t\\ta.refreshOnce(ctx, instance)\\n\\t\\tcase <-timer.C:\\n\\t\\t\\ta.refreshOnce(ctx, instance)\\n\\t\\t}\\n\\t\\tif !timer.Stop() {\\n\\t\\t\\tselect {\\n\\t\\t\\tcase <-timer.C:\\n\\t\\t\\tdefault:\\n\\t\\t\\t}\\n\\t\\t}\\n\\t\\ttimer.Reset(a.refreshInterval(instance.ID))\\n\\t}\\n}\\n```\\n\\nNote: The timer is reset AFTER refreshOnce completes. So the time between two timer-driven refreshOnce starts = refreshOnce duration + interval. Since refreshOnce duration ≤ refreshTimeout (30s, via context timeout, assuming collector respects ctx), the gap ≤ 30s + interval.\\n\\nBut wait, there's a subtlety with the context timeout. In refreshOnce:\\n```go\\nctx, cancel = context.WithTimeout(ctx, refreshTimeout)\\nsnapshot, err := a.collector.Collect(ctx, instance)\\ncancel()\\n```\\n\\nSo the collector is given a ctx with 30s timeout. The CLI collector (`cliCollector`) respects ctx via `exec.CommandContext(ctx, ...)` and its own timeout. So collection is bounded by 30s. Good.\\n\\nBut what about a custom collector that ignores ctx? The comment in app.go line 14-16 says: \\\"A collector should never hold a worker forever. The CLI collector also applies its own command timeout; this bound protects custom collectors.\\\" Hmm, actually the comment says the `refreshTimeout` bound \\\"protects custom collectors\\\" — but if a custom collector ignores ctx, the context.WithTimeout doesn't actually protect anything; the Collect call would block forever. The comment is a bit misleading, but that's pre-existing and not part of this diff.\\n\\nActually wait, the comment is:\\n```go\\n\\t// A collector should never hold a worker forever. The CLI collector also\\n\\t// applies its own command timeout; this bound protects custom collectors.\\n\\trefreshTimeout = 30 * time.Second\\n```\\nThis is pre-existing. The new change adds the freshnessSchedulingSlack comment below. Not part of the review's introduced issues.\\n\\nSo the formula is consistent.\\n\\nNow, one more consideration: the freshnessWindow uses `refreshTimeout` = 30s. But is there a scenario where a legitimate collection could take longer than 30s WITHOUT returning an error? E.g., the CLI collector: if a single subcommand times out at 30s, `runJSON` returns an error → Collect returns error → Err set → stale. So no legitimate success beyond 30s. Good.\\n\\nBut wait — actually the `cliCollector.Collect` runs MULTIPLE subcommands sequentially (version, config show, declaration list, per-declaration show, status). Each subcommand is bounded by c.timeout (30s) AND the parent ctx (30s total). Since the parent ctx from refreshOnce has 30s deadline, the total Collect is bounded by 30s. So no success beyond 30s. Good.\\n\\nActually, let me double-check: `context.WithTimeout(ctx, refreshTimeout)` — ctx here is the parent passed from refreshLoop, which is the app's root ctx (no deadline). So WithTimeout creates a 30s deadline. Then inside Collect, `runJSON` does `context.WithTimeout(ctx, c.timeout)` where ctx is the 30s-deadline ctx. So child deadline = min(30s, now+30s) = 30s. So each subcommand is bounded by the remaining time until 30s. So total Collect ≤ 30s. \\n\\nSo the freshnessWindow = interval + 30s + 1s is exactly right for the production collector.\\n\\nNow let me verify the claim in the commit message more concretely, maybe by checking the git log of the branch or the critic's note. But it's fine.\\n\\nLet me consider whether there are any issues with the test file that could cause flaky CI. The `TestSlowSuccessRefreshRecoversWithoutOverlap` test:\\n\\n- Uses `time.After(5*time.Second)` for several waits, and a busy-spin. Could this be flaky under heavy load? The busy spin loop has a 5s deadline. After release is closed, the second Collect returns immediately, so state should update quickly. Should be fine.\\n\\nBut there's a subtle potential race in the test: After `close(collector.release)`, the first Collect (which was blocked on release) returns. Meanwhile, the test does:\\n```go\\nselect {\\ncase <-collector.entered:\\ncase <-time.After(5 * time.Second):\\n    t.Fatal(\\\"follow-up collection did not start after release\\\")\\n}\\n```\\nThis waits for the SECOND entry into Collect. But note: `entered` is a buffered channel of size 8. The first Collect sent one entry (consumed by the earlier `<-collector.entered`). The second Collect will send another entry. So this select waits for the second. Fine.\\n\\nBut wait — could the first Collect still be running when we `close(collector.release)`? Yes, it's blocked on `<-c.release`. Closing release unblocks it. It then returns `c.snapshot, nil`. Then defer decrements active. Then refreshOnce calls recordRefresh, then returns. Then refreshLoop resets timer and loops. The wake channel has 1 pending. Select picks wake → refreshOnce → second Collect → sends entered (second entry). So the test's second select receives the second entry. Good.\\n\\nActually, there's a subtle question: after the first Collect returns, does the refreshLoop process the wake before the timer? The timer was reset to 300s (won't fire). The wake channel has 1 buffered item. So select will pick wake. Good.\\n\\nSo the test is deterministic enough.\\n\\nNow, is there a concern that the test's busy-wait loop could consume a lot of CPU for up to 5s in a failure scenario? In a failure, the test would spin for 5s then fail. That's acceptable for a test. Not a production issue.\\n\\nNow let me consider code quality of the new test file. The `seedFreshnessSuccess` helper:\\n```go\\nfunc seedFreshnessSuccess(app *App, id string, at time.Time) {\\n\\tapp.mu.Lock()\\n\\tdefer app.mu.Unlock()\\n\\tstate := app.states[id]\\n\\tif state == nil {\\n\\t\\tstate = &InstanceState{}\\n\\t\\tapp.states[id] = state\\n\\t}\\n\\tvar instance Instance\\n\\tfor _, candidate := range app.inventory.Instances {\\n\\t\\tif candidate.ID == id {\\n\\t\\t\\tinstance = candidate\\n\\t\\t\\tbreak\\n\\t\\t}\\n\\t}\\n\\tstate.Snapshot = &Snapshot{}\\n\\tsnapshot := freshnessTestSnapshot(instance)\\n\\tstate.Snapshot = &snapshot\\n\\tstate.LastSuccess = at\\n\\tstate.LastAttempt = at\\n\\tstate.Err = nil\\n}\\n```\\n\\nMinor: `state.Snapshot = &Snapshot{}` is dead assignment. Cosmetic. Also, if the instance ID isn't found, `instance` is zero-value Instance, so the snapshot's Instance field is empty. But all test IDs (\\\"one\\\", \\\"two\\\") exist. Fine.\\n\\nThe helper locks `app.mu` and iterates `app.inventory.Instances` — no lock conflict. Fine.\\n\\nNow, is there a concern that `seedFreshnessSuccess` sets `state.Snapshot` with a pointer to a local `snapshot`? That's fine; Go heap-allocates.\\n\\nNow let me think about whether the change introduces any issue with the `Reachable` field. `Reachable` = `state.Snapshot != nil && state.Err == nil`. It does not depend on freshness. So a stale instance with a snapshot and no error is still \\\"Reachable=true\\\" while \\\"Freshness=stale\\\". That's pre-existing semantics (stale ≠ unreachable). The test doesn't check Reachable. Fine.\\n\\nLet me also verify the whole test suite runs with `-race` to catch any data race in the new tests or production code. The forest.yaml checks don't include race, but as a reviewer I can run it to be thorough. Let me run `go test -race ./...`.\",\"format\":\"unknown\",\"index\":0}]"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_b1732jah6fkt0s6kds8ubx40","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/phaedrus/development/misty-step/canopy/.forest/worktrees/1788551384472524279-verifier && echo \"=== go test -race ./... ===\" && go test -race ./... 2>&1; echo \"EXIT=$?\"","timeout":240}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"openrouter","model":"deepseek/deepseek-v4-pro-0813","usage":{"input":402,"output":1954,"cacheRead":56576,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":1834,"totalTokens":58932,"cost":{"input":0.00026532,"output":0.00386892,"cacheRead":0.0012446719999999998,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.005378912}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1788551683563,"responseId":"gen-1788551683-NbGpIUcXsPv87KmL5xFz","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_b1732jah6fkt0s6kds8ubx40","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"=== go test -race ./... ===\nok  \tcanopy\t1.567s\nEXIT=0\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1788551712313},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Race test passes. Good.\n\nNow let me check the commit's full diff once more for any other subtle issues. I've reviewed the diff. Let me also check whether there's a README or docs that mention the freshness threshold that should be updated. The branch name is \"canopy-critic-stale-selected-window\" and \"canopy-docs-discovery-alignment\" is separate. Let me grep README for freshness mentions.","thinkingSignature":"[{\"type\":\"reasoning.text\",\"text\":\"Race test passes. Good.\\n\\nNow let me check the commit's full diff once more for any other subtle issues. I've reviewed the diff. Let me also check whether there's a README or docs that mention the freshness threshold that should be updated. The branch name is \\\"canopy-critic-stale-selected-window\\\" and \\\"canopy-docs-discovery-alignment\\\" is separate. Let me grep README for freshness mentions.\",\"format\":\"unknown\",\"index\":0}]"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_zsgnx9sit4dm8slowaijaib7","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/phaedrus/development/misty-step/canopy/.forest/worktrees/1788551384472524279-verifier && grep -rn \"stale\\|fresh\\|interval\\|window\\|6s\\|3x\\|3 \\*\" README.md canopy.example.json 2>/dev/null; echo \"---inventory validation---\"; sed -n '1,120p' inventory.go"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"openrouter","model":"deepseek/deepseek-v4-pro-0813","usage":{"input":2137,"output":217,"cacheRead":56832,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":89,"totalTokens":59186,"cost":{"input":0.0014104200000000001,"output":0.00042966,"cacheRead":0.0012503039999999998,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.003090384}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1788551712314,"responseId":"gen-1788551712-GVdXl6OcImBF9wzXqNl8","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_zsgnx9sit4dm8slowaijaib7","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"README.md:9:A failed refresh never becomes an empty healthy state. Canopy retains the last successful snapshot and marks it stale. An instance without a successful snapshot is unknown. Trigger polling and Run outcomes remain separate signals.\nREADME.md:40:  \"fleet_interval_seconds\": 10,\nREADME.md:41:  \"selected_interval_seconds\": 2,\ncanopy.example.json:3:  \"fleet_interval_seconds\": 10,\ncanopy.example.json:4:  \"selected_interval_seconds\": 2,\n---inventory validation---\npackage main\n\nimport (\n\t\"bytes\"\n\t\"encoding/json\"\n\t\"fmt\"\n\t\"io\"\n\t\"net\"\n\t\"os\"\n\t\"path/filepath\"\n\t\"strconv\"\n\t\"strings\"\n\t\"unicode\"\n)\n\nconst (\n\tdefaultListen                  = \"127.0.0.1:8080\"\n\tdefaultFleetIntervalSeconds    = 10\n\tdefaultSelectedIntervalSeconds = 2\n)\n\n// LoadInventory reads and validates one JSON inventory. Unknown fields are\n// rejected so a misspelled operational setting cannot silently select a\n// default; Forest command envelopes have a separate additive-field policy.\nfunc LoadInventory(path string) (Inventory, error) {\n\tif strings.TrimSpace(path) == \"\" {\n\t\treturn Inventory{}, fmt.Errorf(\"inventory path is empty\")\n\t}\n\tcontents, err := os.ReadFile(path)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn Inventory{}, fmt.Errorf(\"read inventory %q: %w\", path, err)\n\t}\n\tdecoder := json.NewDecoder(bytes.NewReader(contents))\n\tdecoder.DisallowUnknownFields()\n\tvar inventory Inventory\n\tif err := decoder.Decode(&inventory); err != nil {\n\t\treturn Inventory{}, fmt.Errorf(\"decode inventory %q: %w\", path, err)\n\t}\n\tvar trailing any\n\tif err := decoder.Decode(&trailing); err != io.EOF {\n\t\tif err == nil {\n\t\t\treturn Inventory{}, fmt.Errorf(\"decode inventory %q: multiple JSON values\", path)\n\t\t}\n\t\treturn Inventory{}, fmt.Errorf(\"decode inventory %q: trailing data: %w\", path, err)\n\t}\n\tif err := validateInventory(&inventory); err != nil {\n\t\treturn Inventory{}, fmt.Errorf(\"validate inventory %q: %w\", path, err)\n\t}\n\treturn inventory, nil\n}\n\nfunc validateInventory(inventory *Inventory) error {\n\tif inventory == nil {\n\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"inventory is nil\")\n\t}\n\tif inventory.Listen == \"\" {\n\t\tinventory.Listen = defaultListen\n\t}\n\tif err := validateListen(inventory.Listen); err != nil {\n\t\treturn err\n\t}\n\tif inventory.FleetIntervalSeconds == 0 {\n\t\tinventory.FleetIntervalSeconds = defaultFleetIntervalSeconds\n\t}\n\tif inventory.SelectedIntervalSeconds == 0 {\n\t\tinventory.SelectedIntervalSeconds = defaultSelectedIntervalSeconds\n\t}\n\tif inventory.FleetIntervalSeconds < 1 {\n\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"fleet_interval_seconds must be positive\")\n\t}\n\tif inventory.SelectedIntervalSeconds < 1 {\n\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"selected_interval_seconds must be positive\")\n\t}\n\tif len(inventory.Instances) == 0 {\n\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"instances must contain at least one instance\")\n\t}\n\tseen := make(map[string]struct{}, len(inventory.Instances))\n\tfor index := range inventory.Instances {\n\t\tinstance := &inventory.Instances[index]\n\t\tif err := validateInstance(*instance); err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"instances[%d]: %w\", index, err)\n\t\t}\n\t\tif _, found := seen[instance.ID]; found {\n\t\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"instances[%d]: duplicate id %q\", index, instance.ID)\n\t\t}\n\t\tseen[instance.ID] = struct{}{}\n\t}\n\treturn nil\n}\n\nfunc validateListen(listen string) error {\n\tif strings.TrimSpace(listen) != listen || strings.ContainsAny(listen, \"\\r\\n\\x00\") {\n\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"listen address %q contains whitespace or control characters\", listen)\n\t}\n\thost, port, err := net.SplitHostPort(listen)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"listen must be host:port: %w\", err)\n\t}\n\tif host == \"\" {\n\t\t// :port is a valid net.Listen address and means all local interfaces.\n\t} else if strings.ContainsAny(host, \" \\t\\r\\n\") {\n\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"listen host %q contains whitespace\", host)\n\t}\n\tportNumber, err := strconv.Atoi(port)\n\tif err != nil || portNumber < 1 || portNumber > 65535 {\n\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"listen port %q is not in 1..65535\", port)\n\t}\n\treturn nil\n}\n\nfunc validateInstance(instance Instance) error {\n\tif err := validateRouteIdentifier(instance.ID, \"instance id\"); err != nil {\n\t\treturn err\n\t}\n\tif strings.TrimSpace(instance.Label) == \"\" {\n\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"instance %q label is empty\", instance.ID)\n\t}\n\tif strings.ContainsRune(instance.Root, '\\x00') {\n\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"instance %q root contains NUL\", instance.ID)\n\t}\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1788551718615},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"README doesn't specify the exact threshold, so no doc change needed. The change is consistent with README line 9's description (\"marks it stale\").\n\nNow let me think about whether the `freshnessWindow` comment/behavior could have a subtle mismatch with the \"selected vs fleet\" semantics.\n\nActually, there is one interesting subtlety: `refreshInterval(id)` uses `a.selected` to determine whether an instance is \"selected\". But `pageView(selectedID, now)` also takes a `selectedID` parameter. Consider the `/fragments/fleet` endpoint or a page view where selectedID differs from `a.selected`.\n\nLet me check `pageView` callers.","thinkingSignature":"[{\"type\":\"reasoning.text\",\"text\":\"README doesn't specify the exact threshold, so no doc change needed. The change is consistent with README line 9's description (\\\"marks it stale\\\").\\n\\nNow let me think about whether the `freshnessWindow` comment/behavior could have a subtle mismatch with the \\\"selected vs fleet\\\" semantics.\\n\\nActually, there is one interesting subtlety: `refreshInterval(id)` uses `a.selected` to determine whether an instance is \\\"selected\\\". But `pageView(selectedID, now)` also takes a `selectedID` parameter. Consider the `/fragments/fleet` endpoint or a page view where selectedID differs from `a.selected`.\\n\\nLet me check `pageView` callers.\",\"format\":\"unknown\",\"index\":0}]"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_7muogdwwpa4bpud9h37w897w","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/phaedrus/development/misty-step/canopy/.forest/worktrees/1788551384472524279-verifier && grep -rn \"pageView\\|selectedID\\|selectInstance\\|IsSelected\" --include=*.go server.go"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"openrouter","model":"deepseek/deepseek-v4-pro-0813","usage":{"input":1432,"output":237,"cacheRead":58880,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":140,"totalTokens":60549,"cost":{"input":0.00094512,"output":0.00046926000000000004,"cacheRead":0.00129536,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.00270974}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1788551718616,"responseId":"gen-1788551718-6SdPKRmsGf5xUedBwjv9","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_7muogdwwpa4bpud9h37w897w","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"64:\t\tid = a.selectedID()\n66:\ta.selectInstance(id)\n67:\tview := a.pageView(id, time.Now().UTC())\n81:\t\ta.selectInstance(id)\n83:\tview := a.pageView(id, time.Now().UTC())\n97:\t\tid = a.selectedID()\n99:\tselectionChanged := a.selectInstance(id)\n103:\tview := a.pageView(id, time.Now().UTC())\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1788551725362},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_aduboe4xnlrs8wwhe8b2ha0j","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/home/phaedrus/development/misty-step/canopy/.forest/worktrees/1788551384472524279-verifier/server.go"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"openrouter","model":"deepseek/deepseek-v4-pro-0813","usage":{"input":490,"output":74,"cacheRead":60160,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":60724,"cost":{"input":0.0003234,"output":0.00014652,"cacheRead":0.00132352,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.0017934399999999999}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1788551725364,"responseId":"gen-1788551725-4qqapoVvhXMTAJUnjH73","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_aduboe4xnlrs8wwhe8b2ha0j","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"package main\n\nimport (\n\t\"bytes\"\n\t\"context\"\n\t\"embed\"\n\t\"errors\"\n\t\"fmt\"\n\t\"html/template\"\n\t\"io/fs\"\n\t\"net/http\"\n\t\"regexp\"\n\t\"time\"\n)\n\n// staticFiles is deliberately embedded at the package boundary: deployments\n// do not depend on a source checkout after the binary is built.\n//\n//go:embed static/*\nvar staticFiles embed.FS\n\nvar (\n\terrMissingInstance = errors.New(\"instance is required\")\n\terrBadInstance     = errors.New(\"invalid instance\")\n\terrUnknownInstance = errors.New(\"unknown instance\")\n\terrMissingRun      = errors.New(\"run is required\")\n\terrBadRun          = errors.New(\"invalid run\")\n)\n\nfunc (a *App) Handler() http.Handler {\n\tstaticRoot, err := fs.Sub(staticFiles, \"static\")\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tpanic(fmt.Sprintf(\"open embedded static files: %v\", err))\n\t}\n\tmux := http.NewServeMux()\n\tmux.HandleFunc(\"/\", a.handlePage)\n\tmux.HandleFunc(\"/fragments/fleet\", a.handleFleet)\n\tmux.HandleFunc(\"/fragments/instance\", a.handleInstance)\n\tmux.HandleFunc(\"/logs\", a.handleLogs)\n\tmux.HandleFunc(\"/healthz\", a.handleHealth)\n\tmux.Handle(\"/static/\", http.HandlerFunc(func(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {\n\t\tif !requireGet(w, r) {\n\t\t\treturn\n\t\t}\n\t\thttp.StripPrefix(\"/static/\", http.FileServer(http.FS(staticRoot))).ServeHTTP(w, r)\n\t}))\n\treturn mux\n}\n\nfunc (a *App) handlePage(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {\n\tif r.URL.Path != \"/\" {\n\t\thttp.NotFound(w, r)\n\t\treturn\n\t}\n\tif !requireGet(w, r) {\n\t\treturn\n\t}\n\tid, err := a.instanceParam(r, false)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\thandleParamError(w, r, err)\n\t\treturn\n\t}\n\tif id == \"\" {\n\t\tid = a.selectedID()\n\t}\n\ta.selectInstance(id)\n\tview := a.pageView(id, time.Now().UTC())\n\ta.render(w, r, []string{\"page.html\", \"page\"}, view)\n}\n\nfunc (a *App) handleFleet(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {\n\tif !requireGet(w, r) {\n\t\treturn\n\t}\n\tid, err := a.instanceParam(r, false)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\thandleParamError(w, r, err)\n\t\treturn\n\t}\n\tif id != \"\" {\n\t\ta.selectInstance(id)\n\t}\n\tview := a.pageView(id, time.Now().UTC())\n\ta.render(w, r, []string{\"fleet.html\", \"fleet\"}, view)\n}\n\nfunc (a *App) handleInstance(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {\n\tif !requireGet(w, r) {\n\t\treturn\n\t}\n\tid, err := a.instanceParam(r, false)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\thandleParamError(w, r, err)\n\t\treturn\n\t}\n\tif id == \"\" {\n\t\tid = a.selectedID()\n\t}\n\tselectionChanged := a.selectInstance(id)\n\tif selectionChanged {\n\t\tw.Header().Set(\"HX-Trigger-After-Swap\", \"canopy-selection\")\n\t}\n\tview := a.pageView(id, time.Now().UTC())\n\ta.render(w, r, []string{\"instance.html\", \"instance\"}, view.Selected)\n}\n\nfunc (a *App) handleLogs(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {\n\tif !requireGet(w, r) {\n\t\treturn\n\t}\n\tinstanceID, err := a.instanceParam(r, true)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\thandleParamError(w, r, err)\n\t\treturn\n\t}\n\trunID, err := runParam(r)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\thandleParamError(w, r, err)\n\t\treturn\n\t}\n\tinstance, ok := a.instance(instanceID)\n\tif !ok {\n\t\t// instanceParam already validates this; retain a defensive boundary for\n\t\t// an App whose inventory is changed by a package-local test.\n\t\thttp.NotFound(w, r)\n\t\treturn\n\t}\n\tif a.collector == nil {\n\t\thttp.Error(w, \"collector is not configured\", http.StatusInternalServerError)\n\t\treturn\n\t}\n\tctx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(r.Context(), refreshTimeout)\n\tresult, collectErr := a.collector.Logs(ctx, instance, runID, false)\n\tcancel()\n\tsanitizedText := sanitizeLogText(result.Text)\n\tview := LogView{RunID: runID, Complete: result.Complete, Retained: result.Retained, Exit: result.Exit, Text: sanitizedText}\n\tswitch {\n\tcase collectErr != nil && isUnknownRunError(collectErr):\n\t\tview.State = \"unknown\"\n\t\tview.Error = collectErr.Error()\n\tcase collectErr != nil:\n\t\tview.State = \"error\"\n\t\tview.Error = collectErr.Error()\n\tcase result.Retained:\n\t\tview.State = \"retained\"\n\tdefault:\n\t\t// The CLI deliberately reports exit 0 + retained=false for a known Run\n\t\t// whose log has been evicted. It is not an HTTP error.\n\t\tview.State = \"evicted\"\n\t}\n\ta.render(w, r, []string{\"log.html\", \"log\"}, view)\n}\n\nfunc (a *App) handleHealth(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {\n\tif !requireGet(w, r) {\n\t\treturn\n\t}\n\tw.Header().Set(\"Content-Type\", \"text/plain; charset=utf-8\")\n\tw.WriteHeader(http.StatusOK)\n\t_, _ = w.Write([]byte(\"ok\\n\"))\n}\n\nfunc (a *App) instanceParam(r *http.Request, required bool) (string, error) {\n\tvalues, present := r.URL.Query()[\"instance\"]\n\tif !present {\n\t\tif required {\n\t\t\treturn \"\", errMissingInstance\n\t\t}\n\t\treturn \"\", nil\n\t}\n\tif len(values) != 1 || values[0] == \"\" || validateRouteIdentifier(values[0], \"instance id\") != nil {\n\t\treturn \"\", errBadInstance\n\t}\n\tif _, ok := a.instance(values[0]); !ok {\n\t\treturn \"\", errUnknownInstance\n\t}\n\treturn values[0], nil\n}\n\nfunc (a *App) instance(id string) (Instance, bool) {\n\ta.mu.RLock()\n\tdefer a.mu.RUnlock()\n\tfor _, instance := range a.inventory.Instances {\n\t\tif instance.ID == id {\n\t\t\treturn instance, true\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn Instance{}, false\n}\n\nfunc runParam(r *http.Request) (string, error) {\n\tvalues, present := r.URL.Query()[\"run\"]\n\tif !present || len(values) != 1 || values[0] == \"\" || validateRouteIdentifier(values[0], \"run id\") != nil {\n\t\tif !present || len(values) == 0 || (len(values) == 1 && values[0] == \"\") {\n\t\t\treturn \"\", errMissingRun\n\t\t}\n\t\treturn \"\", errBadRun\n\t}\n\treturn values[0], nil\n}\n\nfunc isUnknownRunError(err error) bool {\n\tvar cliErr *CLIError\n\treturn errors.As(err, &cliErr) && cliErr != nil && cliErr.Exit == 4\n}\nvar (\n\tkeyPattern = regexp.MustCompile(`(?i)(keys/|key[_-]?id[=:]|token[=:]|bearer\\s+|api[_-]?key[=:])([a-zA-Z0-9_\\-\\.]{12,})`)\n\tbearerPattern = regexp.MustCompile(`(?i)sk-[a-zA-Z0-9_\\-]{20,}`)\n)\n\nfunc sanitizeLogText(text string) string {\n\tif text == \"\" {\n\t\treturn \"\"\n\t}\n\tres := keyPattern.ReplaceAllString(text, \"${1}[REDACTED]\")\n\tres = bearerPattern.ReplaceAllString(res, \"[REDACTED]\")\n\treturn res\n}\n\n\nfunc requireGet(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) bool {\n\tif r.Method == http.MethodGet {\n\t\treturn true\n\t}\n\tw.Header().Set(\"Allow\", http.MethodGet)\n\thttp.Error(w, \"method not allowed\", http.StatusMethodNotAllowed)\n\treturn false\n}\n\nfunc handleParamError(w http.ResponseWriter, _ *http.Request, err error) {\n\tstatus := http.StatusBadRequest\n\tif errors.Is(err, errUnknownInstance) {\n\t\tstatus = http.StatusNotFound\n\t}\n\thttp.Error(w, err.Error(), status)\n}\n\nfunc (a *App) render(w http.ResponseWriter, _ *http.Request, names []string, data any) {\n\tif a.templates == nil {\n\t\thttp.Error(w, \"templates are not configured\", http.StatusInternalServerError)\n\t\treturn\n\t}\n\tvar selected *template.Template\n\tfor _, name := range names {\n\t\tif candidate := a.templates.Lookup(name); candidate != nil {\n\t\t\tselected = candidate\n\t\t\tbreak\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tif selected == nil {\n\t\thttp.Error(w, fmt.Sprintf(\"template %q is not configured\", names[0]), http.StatusInternalServerError)\n\t\treturn\n\t}\n\tvar body bytes.Buffer\n\tif err := selected.Execute(&body, data); err != nil {\n\t\thttp.Error(w, \"render template\", http.StatusInternalServerError)\n\t\treturn\n\t}\n\tw.Header().Set(\"Content-Type\", \"text/html; charset=utf-8\")\n\tw.Header().Set(\"Cache-Control\", \"no-store\")\n\t_, _ = w.Write(body.Bytes())\n}\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1788551729282},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"There's a subtle issue I want to examine: `handleFleet` calls `a.pageView(id, ...)` where `id` may be \"\" (no instance param). In `pageView`, if `selectedID == \"\"`, it sets `selectedID = a.selectedID()`. Then it iterates instances and for each instance, marks `instance.ID == selectedID` as selected. And it calls `a.freshnessWindow(instance.ID)` which internally uses `a.selected` (not `selectedID` parameter) to decide the interval.\n\nSo `freshnessWindow(instance.ID)` uses `a.selected`, while `instanceView(..., instance.ID == selectedID, ...)` uses the `selectedID` parameter. Are these always consistent?\n\nIn `handleFleet`: if id == \"\", then pageView's selectedID = a.selectedID() = a.selected. So `selectedID` param equals a.selected. Consistent.\n\nIf id != \"\" (fleet fragment with instance param), handleFleet calls `a.selectInstance(id)` first, which sets a.selected = id. Then pageView(id). So a.selected == id == selectedID param. Consistent.\n\nIn `handlePage`: id from param or selectedID(); then `a.selectInstance(id)` sets a.selected = id; then pageView(id). Consistent.\n\nIn `handleInstance`: same pattern. Consistent.\n\nSo there's no divergence between `a.selected` and the `selectedID` param in practice. But `freshnessWindow` uses `a.selected` internally, while the view's `IsSelected` uses the param. There is a subtle coupling, but it's consistent in all current callers. And this coupling is pre-existing (the old code `3*a.refreshInterval(instance.ID)` also used `a.selected` internally via refreshInterval). So not introduced by this change.\n\nActually wait — the old code `instanceView(..., now, 3*a.refreshInterval(instance.ID))` also called refreshInterval which reads a.selected. So same coupling existed before. The change only replaces `3*` with `freshnessWindow`. So no new coupling.\n\nNow, one more consideration: `refreshInterval(id)` reads `a.selected`. But when called from `freshnessWindow` inside `pageView`, and `pageView` is also called with a `selectedID` that may not yet be applied to `a.selected` in some edge (e.g., a test calling pageView directly with a different selectedID). But in production, always consistent. Not a bug introduced.\n\nLet me now think about whether there's a blocking finding. So far the change looks correct, well-tested, and both checks pass. Let me think harder about potential regressions or subtle bugs.\n\n**Potential issue A**: The freshness window for the fleet instance is now `fleetInterval + 30s + 1s`. But the fleet refresh loop for a non-selected instance uses `refreshInterval = FleetIntervalSeconds`. If FleetIntervalSeconds is large (e.g., 300s = 5 min), window = 331s (~5.5 min). Previously 900s (15 min). This means a fleet instance that's healthy but whose collection is slow (e.g., each collection takes 30s) would have LastSuccess gap of ~330s, and window 331s. It stays fresh with 1s margin. But if there's any scheduling drift, it might momentarily appear stale right before the next success. This is a soft signal; not blocking.\n\nBut consider a more realistic scenario: a fleet instance with a 10s interval and a collection that takes, say, 5s. Gap between successes = 15s. Window = 41s. Fresh. Fine.\n\n**Potential issue B**: Is the freshness window now too lenient for the selected instance, hiding real staleness for up to 33s? Previously a dead selected instance (2s interval) would be marked stale after 6s. Now it takes 33s. Is 33s too long for an operator to notice a dead worker?\n\nThe design intent (per commit and comments) is that \"stale\" should mean \"exceeded one legitimate cycle.\" A silent worker (dead) is indistinguishable from a slow worker for at most one cycle. The old 6s was incorrect because a healthy worker with a slow collection (up to 30s) would be falsely marked stale. So 33s is the correct bound.\n\nBut is there a tradeoff where a truly dead worker is shown \"fresh\" for 33s and that's operationally dangerous? This is a judgment call. The commit explicitly frames it as a fix for false-stale, and the window matches the actual worst-case cycle. I don't think this is a blocking issue — it's the intended behavior and is well-reasoned.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider the semantics of \"LastSuccess\". `LastSuccess` is updated in `recordRefresh` only on success. But `LastAttempt` is recorded at the START of each refresh. Could the freshness window use `LastAttempt` to be more precise? The comment explicitly says \"LastAttempt never extends this window.\" So the design is: even if the worker is attempting (LastAttempt recent) but hasn't succeeded within the window, it's stale. That's fine — a worker that keeps attempting but keeps succeeding slowly is fine as long as it succeeds within the window; a worker that attempts but never succeeds would have Err set (stale immediately) or, if it somehow doesn't set Err (e.g., a custom collector that blocks forever and ignores ctx), it becomes stale after the window based on LastSuccess. Good.\n\nBut consider: a worker that's healthy and succeeding every cycle. LastSuccess updates each cycle. The gap between successes is interval + collection time ≤ interval + 30s. So it never exceeds the window (interval + 31s). Good.\n\nNow, there's an edge: what if the collection returns success but takes slightly more than 30s because the CLI collector's timeout is 30s but... no, it's bounded.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider the CLI collector's timeout. In main.go, `NewCLICollector(refreshTimeout)` where refreshTimeout=30s. In refreshOnce, the context timeout is also 30s. In `runJSON`, `context.WithTimeout(ctx, c.timeout)` = min(parent 30s, 30s). So each subcommand ≤30s AND whole Collect ≤30s (parent). So max collection 30s. Good.\n\nSo the window is correct.\n\n**Potential issue C**: The new test file introduces helper functions `renderInstanceFreshness` and `renderFleetFreshness` that call `loadTemplates()`. Is `loadTemplates` reading from embedded FS each time? Yes, it parses `templates/*.html` from embed each call. In tests, this is fine (a few calls). Not a concern.\n\n**Potential issue D**: Test naming/placement. `freshness_test.go` is a new test file in package main. It references `testCollector` from app_test.go. Test files in the same package share declarations, so this is fine for `go test`. But it creates a dependency of `freshness_test.go` on `app_test.go`'s `testCollector`. That's normal Go test practice. Fine.\n\n**Potential issue E**: The `slowSuccessCollector` in freshness_test.go defines its own `Collect` and `Logs`. It duplicates `testCollector`'s concurrency-tracking pattern but that's fine for a specialized test.\n\nNow, let me also verify there are no compilation issues with `go build`.","thinkingSignature":"[{\"type\":\"reasoning.text\",\"text\":\"There's a subtle issue I want to examine: `handleFleet` calls `a.pageView(id, ...)` where `id` may be \\\"\\\" (no instance param). In `pageView`, if `selectedID == \\\"\\\"`, it sets `selectedID = a.selectedID()`. Then it iterates instances and for each instance, marks `instance.ID == selectedID` as selected. And it calls `a.freshnessWindow(instance.ID)` which internally uses `a.selected` (not `selectedID` parameter) to decide the interval.\\n\\nSo `freshnessWindow(instance.ID)` uses `a.selected`, while `instanceView(..., instance.ID == selectedID, ...)` uses the `selectedID` parameter. Are these always consistent?\\n\\nIn `handleFleet`: if id == \\\"\\\", then pageView's selectedID = a.selectedID() = a.selec